//! The LLVM text of an exported function with a parameter of two words, by Q3
//! of "Answers of 2026-11-07, third set". C may pass a callback with a context
//! of its own. The function keeps both words in a slot of its frame and holds
//! the thunk `callback.0` and the address of that slot instead. The thunk takes
//! the canonical NaN of `x` and calls the callback with the context of C. It
//! returns the result as the callback gave it, by Q2 of "Answers of 2026-11-07,
//! fourth set", and `run` takes the canonical NaN of it, as every exported
//! function does.

export fn run(f: fn(f32) -> f32, x: f32) -> f32
{
	return f(x);
}

fn main() -> int
{
	return 0;
}
